.TH IN-1 1 "September 2026" "in-1" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
in-1 \- instant dev tools for your current shell


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsource <(curl -sL in-1.cc)\fP \fITOOL\fP[\fB=\fP\fIVERSION\fP]...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fITOOL\fP[\fB=\fP\fIVERSION\fP]...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--local\fP \fITOOL\fP\&...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--list\fP | \fB--upgrade\fP | \fB--version\fP | \fB--help\fP

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--env\fP \fISHELL\fP \fITOOL\fP\&...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--complete\fP \fISHELL\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBin-1\fP installs development tools and languages under a temp root
and puts a single directory, \fI/bin\fP, on \fBPATH\fP for the
current shell session (plus \fBMANPATH\fP and completions).
Nothing else on the system changes.

.PP
Each tool version installs into \fI/share//\fP, and
in-1 writes a small wrapper for every command it provides into
\fI/bin\fP\&.  The wrapper carries the tool's own environment
(CARGO_HOME, ...), so \fBwhich <cmd>\fR is always \fI/bin/\fP and
the shell itself stays clean.  Multiple versions coexist: the primary
command of a tool also gets a version-specific wrapper,
\fI-\fP\&.

.PP
For a session the root is \fIIN1_ROOT\fP; for \fB--local\fP it is \fIPREFIX\fP\&.

.PP
It works in bash, zsh and fish, on Linux and macOS (Intel and ARM),
and needs only \fBgit\fP(1), \fBcurl\fP(1), GNU \fBmake\fP(1) and
\fBbash\fP(1) to be installed.

.PP
Tools are installed by the makes project
(https://github.com/makeplus/makes); any tool with a \fITOOL\fP\&.mk file
there is available.  A tool may also be requested by an alias command
name (e.g. \fBcargo\fP installs rust, \fBbb\fP installs babashka).
Run \fBin-1 --list\fP to see tools and aliases.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--local\fP
  Install the tools under \fIPREFIX\fP (default \fI~/.local\fP, or
  \fI/usr/local\fP when run as root) and write a wrapper for each tool
  command into \fIPREFIX/bin\fP\&.
  These installs persist across shell sessions and need no shell
  setup, since the wrappers carry the tools' environment.

.PP
\fB--list\fP
  List all available tool names and command aliases.

.PP
\fB--env\fP \fISHELL\fP
  Install the tools, then print environment setup code for \fISHELL\fP
  (\fBbash\fP, \fBzsh\fP or \fBfish\fP) on stdout.
  This is the plumbing used by the sourced one-liner and by the
  \fBin-1\fP shell function; you can use it directly:
  \fBeval "$(in-1 --env bash rust)"\fP

.PP
\fB--complete\fP \fISHELL\fP
  Print tab completion code for the \fBin-1\fP command itself.

.PP
\fB--upgrade\fP
  Update the in-1 repo clone to the latest version (or to
  \fIIN1_VERSION\fP if set).

.PP
\fB--version\fP
  Print the in-1 version.

.PP
\fB--help\fP
  Print usage help.


.SH ENVIRONMENT
\fBIN1_ROOT\fP
  Session install root.
  Default: \fI$TMPDIR/in-1\fP, falling back to \fI/tmp/in-1\fP\&.

.PP
\fBIN1_CACHE\fP
  Download cache directory.
  Default: \fI$IN1_ROOT/.cache\fP\&.

.PP
\fBPREFIX\fP
  Install root override.
  Session default: \fIIN1_ROOT\fP; \fB--local\fP default: \fI~/.local\fP, or
  \fI/usr/local\fP when root.  A relative path is anchored to the current
  directory.

.PP
\fBIN1_VERSION\fP
  The in-1 git ref to use.

.PP
\fBIN1_REPO\fP
  The in-1 repo URL to clone.
  Default: \fIhttps://github.com/in-1-cc/in-1\fP\&.

.PP
\fBIN1_MAKES_REPO\fP
  The makes repo URL to clone.
  Default: \fIhttps://github.com/makeplus/makes\fP\&.

.PP
\fBIN1_UPDATE\fP
  Set to 1 to update the makes clone before installing.

.PP
\fBIN1_VERBOSE\fP
  Set to 1 to stream the full install output instead of the quiet
  per-tool progress lines.

.PP
\fBIN1_TOOLS\fP
  Set by in-1 in the shell; lists the active tools.


.SH EXAMPLES
Try Rust and Node in the current shell:

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) rust node
.EE

.PP
Same, from fish:

.EX
curl -sL in-1.cc | source - rust node
.EE

.PP
Pin a version (both \fBgo\fR and \fBgo-1.23.4\fR end up on PATH):

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) go=1.23.4
.EE

.PP
Install a tool by an alias command name:

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) cargo
.EE

.PP
Install the in-1 command permanently:

.EX
git clone https://github.com/in-1-cc/in-1 ~/.in-1
echo 'source ~/.in-1/.rc' >> ~/.bashrc
.EE

.PP
Keep jq around for good:

.EX
in-1 --local jq
.EE


